1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:12,000
They are the tools of a dangerous trade employed by men and women who risk their lives to bear witness to war.

2
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
Now, Frontline reporting on Modern Marvels.

3
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:31,001
In March 2003, a military convoy rolled across the desert in Iraq, the first wave of a ground

4
00:00:35,680 --> 00:00:39,880
attack by the United States against the regime of Saddam Hussein.

5
00:00:39,881 --> 00:00:45,840
But along with soldiers and their weapons of war, the convoy carried civilian correspondence

6
00:00:45,841 --> 00:00:48,400
in the vanguard of another revolution.

7
00:00:48,401 --> 00:00:52,220
Equipped with satellite phones, video phones, and portable uplinks,

8
00:00:52,221 --> 00:00:56,020
these frontline reporters could transmit the sounds and sights of war

9
00:00:56,021 --> 00:01:00,720
in ways never previously employed on a battlefield.

10
00:01:00,721 --> 00:01:05,820
Covering war, like waging war, has always depended on speed and flexibility.

11
00:01:05,821 --> 00:01:10,660
For the embedded reporters, equipment light and compact enough to keep pace with the troops

12
00:01:10,661 --> 00:01:15,200
made it possible to communicate in real time from moving battle zones.

13
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:21,480
If it was time to leave, it was time to leave, and there would be no waiting around for the TV people to gather all our things.

14
00:01:21,481 --> 00:01:24,440
That put us under a little bit of a constriction and a time limitation,

15
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:26,820
because we didn't know when they were just going to pick up and leave.

16
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:32,700
Sam Sambatero was part of a TV crew whose equipment included a portable satellite uplink,

17
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:37,420
which could be set up or disassembled like an umbrella within 20 minutes for live broadcast.

18
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:44,180
We were able to shoot our video, capture it onto the laptops, edit it if we needed to,

19
00:01:44,181 --> 00:01:47,360
and send those compressed files back on the satellites.

20
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:50,560
When our colonel would decide where we were going to overnight,

21
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:53,280
I would set the dish up, find the satellite,

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,060
get power from the military, and away we would go.

23
00:01:58,560 --> 00:02:00,280
To broadcast even more quickly,

24
00:02:00,840 --> 00:02:04,200
TV crews also used a video phone known as the Talking Head.

25
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:07,680
Its choppy low bandwidth signal could be transmitted

26
00:02:07,681 --> 00:02:09,620
by one or two satellite telephones

27
00:02:09,621 --> 00:02:12,080
with portable flat-screen antennas.

28
00:02:12,081 --> 00:02:12,907
Hello, General.

29
00:02:12,908 --> 00:02:18,200
We're with the 1st Battalion, 7th Marines, moving in a convoy that now is paused for the moment,

30
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:23,200
but slowly making our way towards the extreme southeast suburbs of Baghdad.

31
00:02:23,440 --> 00:02:27,220
It wasn't as clear as a satellite image could be, but it was pretty good.

32
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:34,160
And when things started to happen, it was a very live connection to the audience, and many people would remark on that.

33
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:36,380
I felt like I was really there with you.

34
00:02:36,381 --> 00:02:44,540
If I wave my hand here, you'll see quite a bit of delay before it makes it 22,000 miles up to the satellite and back down,

35
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:51,300
where it's being downlinked in France, under the ocean, and up here in New York, and it comes out here.

36
00:02:54,180 --> 00:03:02,960
NBC's David Bloom combined the high-quality transmission of a stationary uplink and the mobility of a videophone with the Bloom mobile.

37
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:10,640
Its mounted uplink could transmit full bandwidth video in real time, even while traveling at 60 miles an hour.

38
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:18,860
David's main concern was for him to be able to come live from the battlefield in whatever vehicle he was riding in.

39
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:27,620
While David was moving in a military vehicle, he was able to transmit back to us via microwave and we were able to uplink.

40
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:33,140
The Blue Mobile's key component for transmitting video on the move was its mounted uplink,

41
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:36,580
encased by a dome to control temperature and keep out debris.

42
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:45,100
The uplink maintained its connection to an overhead satellite by pivoting gyroscopically atop a device known as a gimbal mount.

43
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:50,840
Computer-directed movements adjusted its angle as the Blue Mobile sped across the desert.

44
00:03:51,640 --> 00:03:54,700
It's almost like a hip socket where it's free-floating

45
00:03:54,701 --> 00:04:00,860
and is driven by motors that control it so that it can always maintain correct position.

46
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:04,780
But while innovations like the Blue Mobile signal a quantum leap

47
00:04:04,781 --> 00:04:07,200
in the speed and sophistication of frontline reporting,

48
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:10,940
they are only the latest in a litany of technological innovations

49
00:04:10,941 --> 00:04:14,500
which have shaped that profession and our perception of war.

50
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:17,500
And although the tools of the trade keep changing,

51
00:04:17,501 --> 00:04:21,760
The historic mission of frontline reporting has remained remarkably constant.

52
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:25,900
A reporter is doing the same thing he or she has always done,

53
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:29,740
getting the story and communicating it to the people,

54
00:04:30,180 --> 00:04:35,620
either by a typewriter or a computer, or now by satellite and voice.

55
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:40,020
But their basic fundamental job is still to get the facts and report them.

56
00:04:41,260 --> 00:04:45,200
Reports from the front lines of war are nearly as old as war itself.

57
00:04:45,201 --> 00:04:49,640
For centuries, those chronicles were the exclusive province of military men.

58
00:04:50,520 --> 00:04:56,380
Usually written long after the fact, the Greek general Thucydides set the standard in the 4th century BC

59
00:04:56,381 --> 00:04:59,460
with his eight-volume history of the Peloponnesian War.

60
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:05,520
Julius Caesar's accounts of Roman battles inspired a lineage of soldier scribes

61
00:05:05,521 --> 00:05:09,260
that include both Napoleon and his chief adversary, the Duke of Wellington.

62
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:16,720
Dispatches from the Boer War in 1899 helped make the reputation of a young British officer named Winston Churchill.

63
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:21,720
These kinds of guys were writing their own histories, and of course to the winner goes the spoils,

64
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:24,740
and they were writing history as they would like it to have been written.

65
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:27,820
Pictures brought another dimension to these accounts.

66
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:33,800
The story of the Battle of Hastings in 1066 was literally woven into the Bayeux tapestry.

67
00:05:33,801 --> 00:05:39,800
Later, the invention of the printing press abetted the mass production of war-related etchings.

68
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:48,940
Paul Revere's engraving of the Boston Massacre in 1770 helped spark resistance to British rule and fanned the flames of an incipient revolution.

69
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:56,080
With the rise of newspapers and democracy, civilian war correspondents began to appear in the middle of the 19th century.

70
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:01,500
London Times reporter William Howard Russell set the standard for those to follow.

71
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:04,740
Covering the Crimean War in 1854,

72
00:06:05,580 --> 00:06:07,700
he revealed that British troops were poorly led

73
00:06:07,701 --> 00:06:09,920
and lacked rudimentary medical support.

74
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:13,340
His vivid reports of military ineptitude,

75
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:16,260
including the suicidal charge of the Light Brigade,

76
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:18,120
caused a sensation in London,

77
00:06:18,780 --> 00:06:21,960
inspiring Florence Nightingale to lead a nursing service into the field,

78
00:06:22,620 --> 00:06:23,780
bringing down the Prime Minister,

79
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:26,300
and selling newspapers by the box load.

80
00:06:27,100 --> 00:06:29,240
Frontline reporting would never be the same.

81
00:06:30,140 --> 00:06:31,920
For the first time, there's a large contradiction

82
00:06:31,921 --> 00:06:34,380
and a gap between the official version of things

83
00:06:34,381 --> 00:06:36,200
and what somebody who was on site,

84
00:06:36,320 --> 00:06:37,840
someone who was there was actually observing.

85
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:39,160
And that was revolutionary.

86
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:42,520
In Crimea, Russell wrote with a quill pen

87
00:06:42,521 --> 00:06:45,400
and sent his dispatches by horseback, train, or ship.

88
00:06:46,440 --> 00:06:50,360
But by the time he arrived to cover the American Civil War in 1861,

89
00:06:50,361 --> 00:06:53,100
the logistics of his craft had been transformed

90
00:06:53,101 --> 00:06:56,140
by an astonishing invention, the telegraph.

91
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:59,920
A coded electrical signal could travel at nearly the speed of light.

92
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:04,620
And with 50,000 miles of wire set up across the eastern states for that purpose,

93
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:06,780
its primacy was undeniable.

94
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:11,220
As hundreds of reporters flooded the battlefield, eager to scoop the competition,

95
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:13,800
the telegraph altered the style of their work.

96
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:18,360
Since speed was essential, the climax of their stories came first,

97
00:07:18,361 --> 00:07:21,900
the beginning of the so-called inverted pyramid style of reporting.

98
00:07:22,760 --> 00:07:24,200
The technology is new.

99
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,540
a line could be cut at any moment, you could lose the transmission,

100
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:34,000
and reporters are encouraged to get as much of the most important information,

101
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:37,720
the punchline, if you will, in at the top as they possibly can.

102
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:42,560
And that's really the beginning of the format of news that we have to this day.

103
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:47,380
The Telegraph also helped introduce Frontline Reporting's historic counterweight,

104
00:07:47,960 --> 00:07:48,740
government censorship.

105
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:52,820
The Office of President Lincoln's Secretary of War, Edwin Stanton,

106
00:07:52,821 --> 00:07:57,120
manned all northern telegraph stations and examined each dispatch.

107
00:07:57,760 --> 00:08:01,520
Stanton also suspended newspapers and banned correspondence from the front.

108
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:06,780
By 1864, he was issuing his own reports to newspapers in the form of a war diary.

109
00:08:07,700 --> 00:08:12,960
The government was very proactive in trying to influence, I suppose today we would call it spin,

110
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:19,360
but they censored heavily, they put out a lot of releases,

111
00:08:19,361 --> 00:08:24,360
They tried to catalog the successes on the battlefield.

112
00:08:24,361 --> 00:08:28,360
Reporters preferred covering battles with easy proximity to a telegraph station,

113
00:08:28,361 --> 00:08:34,360
ensuring that campaigns such as those led by General Ulysses Grant, close to the nation's capital,

114
00:08:34,361 --> 00:08:39,360
received more coverage than those further afield, such as Sherman's march through Georgia.

115
00:08:39,361 --> 00:08:43,360
The reporters were joined at the front by photographers like Matthew Brady,

116
00:08:43,361 --> 00:08:46,360
who utilized a wagon as a portable darkroom.

117
00:08:46,361 --> 00:08:47,061
room.

118
00:08:47,062 --> 00:08:52,660
Since newspapers of the time lacked the ability to print photographs, Brady exhibited

119
00:08:52,661 --> 00:08:54,254
his in urban galleries.

120
00:08:54,255 --> 00:08:58,620
His cameras required exposures between 3 and 60 seconds, making

121
00:08:58,621 --> 00:09:00,391
action photos impossible.

122
00:09:00,392 --> 00:09:05,280
So Brady and others altered still scenes for dramatic effect, placing

123
00:09:05,281 --> 00:09:10,904
a rifle beside a corpse and dragging dead bodies to more picturesque locations.

124
00:09:10,905 --> 00:09:11,605
Brady

125
00:09:11,606 --> 00:09:14,740
is accused of taking a hand and planting it strategically

126
00:09:14,741 --> 00:09:19,400
so that the body or the look of the scene was gruesome enough.

127
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:22,200
And to their minds, they were thinking,

128
00:09:22,340 --> 00:09:25,220
well, this actually reflects what happened in this battle.

129
00:09:27,120 --> 00:09:29,820
Newspapers also value drama over accuracy.

130
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:32,460
As one Chicago Times editor told reporters,

131
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,900
telegraph fully all news you can get,

132
00:09:34,901 --> 00:09:37,340
and when there is no news, send rumors.

133
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:40,200
The reporting in the Civil War era,

134
00:09:40,201 --> 00:09:43,980
right after the telegraph comes in is particularly sensational.

135
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:45,720
It's particularly bad.

136
00:09:46,160 --> 00:09:49,080
It's not a proud chapter in American journalism.

137
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:54,480
And I think the reason is this feeling of experimentation with the new.

138
00:09:55,040 --> 00:09:58,900
For all that, the Civil War proved conclusively that war was good for business.

139
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:02,700
News of a battle could quintuple a paper's circulation.

140
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:08,880
So in decades to follow, newspapers sent reporters to cover conflicts in far-flung locales.

141
00:10:08,881 --> 00:10:11,920
while publishers such as William Randolph Hearst

142
00:10:11,921 --> 00:10:14,220
provided enthusiastic support.

143
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:18,500
Lobbying for an American entry to the Cuban Revolution in 1897,

144
00:10:19,340 --> 00:10:21,580
Hearst sent illustrator Frederick Remington to Havana

145
00:10:21,581 --> 00:10:22,840
with these instructions.

146
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:26,480
You furnish the pictures, I'll furnish the war.

147
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:30,360
Once it began, Richard Harding Davis' dramatic account

148
00:10:30,361 --> 00:10:32,620
of Teddy Roosevelt's charge up San Juan Hill

149
00:10:32,621 --> 00:10:36,060
helped fuel the future president's political career.

150
00:10:36,061 --> 00:10:42,300
There was a swashbuckling quality to the correspondents.

151
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:45,280
They were really the first media celebrities.

152
00:10:45,860 --> 00:10:48,000
They loved the role of war correspondents,

153
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:53,660
much as Teddy Roosevelt relished the role of swashbuckling soldier.

154
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:57,300
The power of the press was able to elevate people

155
00:10:57,301 --> 00:10:59,760
to a much larger degree than there is today.

156
00:10:59,980 --> 00:11:02,960
The country was smaller, newspapers carried much more weight,

157
00:11:02,961 --> 00:11:04,420
the influence was profound,

158
00:11:04,421 --> 00:11:09,180
and there was a political agenda in almost all circumstances.

159
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:12,100
By the close of the 19th century,

160
00:11:12,560 --> 00:11:16,520
front-line reporting reflected both the popularity and the power of the press.

161
00:11:17,120 --> 00:11:20,680
In decades to come, two world wars and new technology

162
00:11:20,681 --> 00:11:24,220
would reveal the range and the limits of that power.

163
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:29,960
The Battle of Antietam in 1862

164
00:11:29,961 --> 00:11:33,460
marked the first time the dead soldiers were photographed on the field

165
00:11:33,461 --> 00:11:34,880
after a major conflict.

166
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:38,240
Frontline reporting will return on Modern Marvels.

167
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:44,320
The dawn of the 20th century coincided with remarkable advances in communications.

168
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:48,000
The telephone transmitted speech over electric wires,

169
00:11:48,480 --> 00:11:51,580
while the wireless conveyed messages over open space.

170
00:11:52,020 --> 00:11:54,640
Still cameras could freeze action from 600 yards,

171
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:57,200
while motion picture cameras captured movement.

172
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:01,260
All were in place in 1914, when World War I began,

173
00:12:01,261 --> 00:12:04,300
but their uses would be severely constricted on the battlefield.

174
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:07,760
World War I was basically censored.

175
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:12,900
There was very little contemporaneous coverage of it.

176
00:12:13,260 --> 00:12:15,720
Much of the technology was available

177
00:12:15,721 --> 00:12:18,780
and might have made for some fascinating coverage

178
00:12:18,781 --> 00:12:22,260
of a very, very bloody conflict.

179
00:12:23,740 --> 00:12:26,240
We just, not much of it was seen.

180
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,420
The burgeoning technology of war itself,

181
00:12:29,421 --> 00:12:31,740
including machine guns and poison gas,

182
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:34,180
produced an unprecedented carnage

183
00:12:34,181 --> 00:12:36,860
that government officials weren't keen to make public.

184
00:12:38,320 --> 00:12:41,400
England's war office refused at first to credential reporters at all.

185
00:12:42,820 --> 00:12:46,260
In France, photographers were banned from taking pictures at the front,

186
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,100
under penalty of death.

187
00:12:49,180 --> 00:12:51,800
Staged photographs and mock newsreel footage of battles

188
00:12:51,801 --> 00:12:53,320
were often shown instead.

189
00:12:54,340 --> 00:12:56,720
But war correspondents from neutral countries,

190
00:12:57,140 --> 00:12:58,700
including at first the United States,

191
00:12:58,701 --> 00:13:00,500
had somewhat more free reign.

192
00:13:01,080 --> 00:13:02,800
Though most were still banned from the front,

193
00:13:03,260 --> 00:13:06,700
American reporters could telegraph dispatches to their readers across the ocean,

194
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:08,900
thanks to another remarkable innovation.

195
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:11,840
The transatlantic cable made a huge difference

196
00:13:11,841 --> 00:13:14,940
because people obviously were able to file stories for the first time

197
00:13:14,941 --> 00:13:19,080
that kept the Americans abreast of what was going on thousands of miles away.

198
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:22,000
Essentially a long-distance telegraph wire,

199
00:13:22,580 --> 00:13:26,140
a typical undersea cable was comprised of strands of pure copper wire

200
00:13:26,141 --> 00:13:29,060
insulated by a natural gum called gutta percha

201
00:13:29,061 --> 00:13:32,340
and surrounded by layers of steel wire and jute yarn.

202
00:13:32,860 --> 00:13:36,500
By 1914, there were 15 such cables spanning the Atlantic

203
00:13:36,501 --> 00:13:39,780
at a minimum distance of about 1,900 miles.

204
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:43,220
Many of them laid across a gently sloping seabed

205
00:13:43,221 --> 00:13:45,020
known as the Atlantic Plateau.

206
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:48,180
Since a cabled dispatch could cost hundreds of dollars,

207
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:51,360
press associations were formed to share expenses.

208
00:13:51,540 --> 00:13:54,040
This led to widespread dissemination of news

209
00:13:54,041 --> 00:13:56,500
as it hadn't really ever been accomplished before.

210
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,600
Once America entered the war in 1917, however,

211
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:02,500
U.S. officials censored heavily as well.

212
00:14:03,540 --> 00:14:06,660
Newspapers were required to post a $10,000 bond,

213
00:14:07,100 --> 00:14:09,180
which could be confiscated for critical coverage.

214
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:11,640
Major events in the field were ignored.

215
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:15,040
When thousands of American troops were sent from France to Russia

216
00:14:15,041 --> 00:14:18,120
in an unsuccessful effort to quell the Bolshevik Revolution,

217
00:14:18,660 --> 00:14:19,820
the news went unreported.

218
00:14:20,680 --> 00:14:23,700
If people are not allowed to report the war,

219
00:14:23,701 --> 00:14:27,640
you get whatever reporting is available, and mostly that means it's not going to be very good.

220
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:33,000
The telegraph, the telephone, and the undersea cable all made point-to-point contact.

221
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:37,820
But radio, whose popularity grew rapidly in the years following World War I,

222
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:42,260
could send its signal to millions at once, inspiring the term broadcasting.

223
00:14:43,420 --> 00:14:46,960
Broadcasting was actually a term used in agriculture.

224
00:14:47,460 --> 00:14:51,120
It was to spread a seed, to cast it broadly.

225
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:57,120
And it is in World War II that it begins to have that effect on the nation.

226
00:14:57,440 --> 00:14:59,900
It's the first national medium.

227
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:03,300
It was a radio commentator, Edward R. Murrow,

228
00:15:03,500 --> 00:15:07,180
whose broadcasts from London during the German air blitz in the fall of 1940

229
00:15:07,181 --> 00:15:10,240
helped tilt American sentiment toward the Allied cause.

230
00:15:11,180 --> 00:15:13,600
Once the U.S. entered the war in 1941,

231
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:17,520
thousands of radio and print reporters, cameramen and photographers

232
00:15:17,521 --> 00:15:21,920
merged into the first wave of officially embedded war correspondents.

233
00:15:22,960 --> 00:15:25,440
The press wore uniforms, underwent military training,

234
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:27,340
and accompanied troops to battle.

235
00:15:28,540 --> 00:15:30,960
This was a war that needed to have the support at home,

236
00:15:30,961 --> 00:15:33,520
and because of that, the reports had much more access

237
00:15:33,521 --> 00:15:35,360
in a way that they didn't have before.

238
00:15:36,260 --> 00:15:39,600
We were treated as major grade officers,

239
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:42,820
so we had the use of officers' quarters,

240
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:46,220
officers' communication facilities, personal communication,

241
00:15:46,221 --> 00:15:47,560
and that sort of thing.

242
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:51,020
As the military depended on reporters to tell its stories,

243
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:54,360
reporters often depended on the military to deliver them.

244
00:15:55,460 --> 00:15:57,720
After typing copy from behind the front lines,

245
00:15:58,300 --> 00:16:00,740
they sent it to press headquarters via military courier.

246
00:16:01,560 --> 00:16:04,220
Other methods of delivery in Europe varied with location.

247
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:10,240
Of course, there is normally no civilian communication facility available.

248
00:16:10,860 --> 00:16:13,640
So it was whatever the military made available.

249
00:16:13,641 --> 00:16:19,480
from the Navy that was sent by the Navy radio communications as far as the roller.

250
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:23,760
From the land standpoint, we filed through the Army,

251
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:28,760
and again the military used their facilities to send our material back.

252
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:34,060
Motion picture, film, and photographs were usually processed at military centers as well.

253
00:16:35,660 --> 00:16:39,980
Cameras and typewriters remained the standard tools in the field, despite radio's popularity.

254
00:16:40,780 --> 00:16:46,340
Short wave transmissions could be obliterated by storms or sunspots and were generally unreliable.

255
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:53,120
Reporters who were broadcasting from the field, from the battlefield, from a war zone,

256
00:16:53,220 --> 00:16:56,420
had more difficulty and were more prone to weather problems.

257
00:16:56,720 --> 00:17:00,260
Despite the close relationship between the American military and the press,

258
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:04,160
every dispatch was passed through sensors before being shipped home.

259
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:09,140
In return, reporters got unprecedented access to briefings and strategy

260
00:17:09,141 --> 00:17:10,900
and an up-close view of the war.

261
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:14,540
Censorship, I thought, was very intelligently handled

262
00:17:14,541 --> 00:17:16,580
in England during the war.

263
00:17:17,400 --> 00:17:21,280
In 1943, eight London-based correspondents,

264
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:22,660
including Walter Cronkite,

265
00:17:23,340 --> 00:17:25,200
became the first group of U.S. reporters

266
00:17:25,201 --> 00:17:27,020
to fly bombing missions over Germany.

267
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:29,520
Though officially non-combatants,

268
00:17:29,960 --> 00:17:32,060
they were taught how to survive in high altitude,

269
00:17:32,500 --> 00:17:35,680
how to bail out, and how to fire a .50-caliber machine gun.

270
00:17:36,420 --> 00:17:38,560
Correspondents are supposed to be non-combatants

271
00:17:38,561 --> 00:17:40,560
that are not supposed to carry weapons.

272
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:43,400
But the thought was with the Air Force, I think,

273
00:17:43,960 --> 00:17:47,160
that if they needed us on a gun,

274
00:17:47,860 --> 00:17:51,280
how were the Germans going to know whether we'd been firing a gun or not

275
00:17:51,281 --> 00:17:52,920
if we had to bail out of that airplane?

276
00:17:53,580 --> 00:17:58,000
For reporters, there was no lack of danger or courage on the front lines of war.

277
00:17:58,001 --> 00:18:03,040
On D-Day in 1944, some parachuted in with typewriters strapped on their backs.

278
00:18:03,640 --> 00:18:06,880
Photographers slipped condoms over their film packs to seal out moisture

279
00:18:06,881 --> 00:18:10,900
as they waded ashore on Omaha Beach in the midst of enemy fire.

280
00:18:11,780 --> 00:18:15,460
As someone wrote down on the battle, landing at Normandy,

281
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:19,580
the brave ones were shooting the enemy and the crazy ones were shooting film.

282
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:22,400
Reporting wasn't limited to civilians.

283
00:18:23,460 --> 00:18:28,240
Photographers like the Army Air Corps' Jerry Cole shot pictures for both military and the public

284
00:18:28,241 --> 00:18:32,480
with a K-24 camera he installed in the ball turret of a B-17 bomber.

285
00:18:33,340 --> 00:18:39,300
Being down the ball turret suspended underneath the B-17 was almost like having your own plane.

286
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:44,660
You could point any direction you wanted without having to tell the pilot to change course.

287
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:46,760
And it was absolutely fantastic.

288
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:53,020
And, of course, knowing that you had two .50 caliber machine guns there was also kind of exciting.

289
00:18:53,320 --> 00:18:58,220
Cole also shot photos from the B-17 by sticking his camera out an open window of the plane

290
00:18:58,221 --> 00:19:02,140
while traveling at high altitude at 200 miles an hour.

291
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:10,620
What you do is move into the window like this and go out far enough until you felt the slipstream hit and your camera would sort of go like that.

292
00:19:10,900 --> 00:19:15,320
And you just come back about an inch or two and you can take your pictures.

293
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:24,060
This is the camera now in the shooting position where I have the ball centered in the crosshairs.

294
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:29,140
And you're very fortunate that I'm using a camera and not twin .50 caliber machine guns.

295
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:37,480
the invention of wire photo and radio photo services boosted the impact of war photography.

296
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:43,480
A photoelectric cell could read a picture as it revolved on a horizontal cylinder,

297
00:19:43,481 --> 00:19:46,480
then send that transmission over phone lines or wireless.

298
00:19:46,481 --> 00:19:49,480
The result was nationwide photo news.

299
00:19:50,480 --> 00:19:54,480
Photographer Joe Rosenthal never saw the iconic image he captured

300
00:19:54,481 --> 00:19:58,480
of American soldiers raising the flag during the Battle of Iwo Jima.

301
00:19:58,481 --> 00:20:02,020
He simply sent his film to a military press center at Guam,

302
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:04,700
where it was processed and transmitted to the mainland,

303
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:07,100
appearing in hundreds of papers the next day.

304
00:20:09,360 --> 00:20:12,300
Published photographs became more graphic as the war progressed.

305
00:20:13,200 --> 00:20:15,660
At first, pictures of dead U.S. soldiers were prohibited.

306
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:19,220
In a bid to boost public support for the war effort, however,

307
00:20:19,960 --> 00:20:22,680
President Roosevelt lifted those restrictions in 1943.

308
00:20:22,681 --> 00:20:32,680
It was okay to photograph pictures of the corpses of Germans and the Japanese soldiers, but not Americans.

309
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:41,540
And Roosevelt felt that perhaps it was time to show Americans the grisly nature of what American soldiers were undergoing.

310
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:44,960
World War II would be remembered as the Good War,

311
00:20:45,520 --> 00:20:50,980
a conflict in which political and military aims blended seamlessly with the duties of a free press.

312
00:20:50,981 --> 00:20:54,220
that harmony would be shattered in conflicts to come.

313
00:20:58,120 --> 00:21:02,280
558 correspondents were accredited to cover the D-Day invasion in 1944,

314
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:06,800
filing more than 700,000 words of copy in one day.

315
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:09,400
Each wrote their obituary before departing.

316
00:21:10,240 --> 00:21:12,440
Frontline Reporting will return on Modern Marvels.

317
00:21:15,020 --> 00:21:19,420
The end of World War II, the Good War, bequeathed the Cold War.

318
00:21:20,380 --> 00:21:25,060
That included a series of proxy conflicts backed by the United States and the Soviet Union

319
00:21:25,061 --> 00:21:28,540
and covered by a new mass medium, television.

320
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:34,420
Television was established in 10 million American homes by 1950,

321
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:36,700
when U.S. troops went to war in Korea.

322
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:40,220
But that conflict, which took place on harsh, forbidding terrain,

323
00:21:40,680 --> 00:21:43,640
far from major cities and with few communications links,

324
00:21:43,641 --> 00:21:45,520
made timely coverage difficult.

325
00:21:46,060 --> 00:21:48,660
Transporting filmed reports could take days, even weeks.

326
00:21:49,420 --> 00:21:52,180
Partly as a result, TV coverage was all but absent.

327
00:21:53,100 --> 00:21:58,540
It was called a forgotten war in part because it was very difficult for reporters to do something with this war

328
00:21:58,541 --> 00:22:01,460
and to cover it in the same kinds of ways they could cover other conflicts.

329
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:04,840
It was very hard to get there. It was hard to get copy back.

330
00:22:05,820 --> 00:22:09,760
And it's not clear that the public had much appetite for hearing about it.

331
00:22:10,460 --> 00:22:12,000
Vietnam was another story.

332
00:22:12,280 --> 00:22:15,860
As the U.S. military presence grew in the early 1960s,

333
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:18,940
the three major TV networks opened bureaus in Saigon.

334
00:22:18,941 --> 00:22:23,180
and expanded their nightly newscasts from 15 to 30 minutes.

335
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:27,660
We did take the war to people's dinner tables.

336
00:22:28,460 --> 00:22:32,940
We had film almost every night of battle circumstances.

337
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:35,800
We had several cameramen in the field,

338
00:22:36,620 --> 00:22:40,780
and there was scarcely a major action that we didn't have some film on,

339
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:43,380
maybe not the first day, but within the second day.

340
00:22:43,381 --> 00:22:48,720
People thought they were seeing war in real time.

341
00:22:48,940 --> 00:22:54,960
The sense of watching war in real time was an illusion.

342
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:59,000
It took between two to four days for 16mm film to be shot,

343
00:22:59,500 --> 00:23:02,100
flown to New York, processed and edited for airing.

344
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:05,540
A TV field crew typically consisted of a correspondent

345
00:23:05,541 --> 00:23:08,780
and a film cameraman and audio engineer team,

346
00:23:09,160 --> 00:23:10,360
literally linked by cables.

347
00:23:11,380 --> 00:23:14,800
The cumbersome arrangement could be dangerous when bullets began to fly.

348
00:23:15,580 --> 00:23:19,040
We had two umbilical cables, a short one and a long one.

349
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:21,740
Short one was about 20 feet, long one was 40 feet.

350
00:23:22,040 --> 00:23:25,620
If you did get into a firefight, which you're bound to,

351
00:23:25,740 --> 00:23:27,540
because otherwise you wouldn't be out there,

352
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:30,480
you'd usually use the 40-footer.

353
00:23:30,880 --> 00:23:33,460
So if you and your sound man got separated,

354
00:23:33,461 --> 00:23:36,920
he was behind one tree if you're in the jungle, you were behind another,

355
00:23:37,140 --> 00:23:40,440
the cable would stretch, hence sound throughout.

356
00:23:40,840 --> 00:23:44,460
Once a battle began, the hefty mouse-eared film camera

357
00:23:44,461 --> 00:23:46,760
made an inviting target for enemy troops.

358
00:23:47,300 --> 00:23:49,620
They didn't know what TV is.

359
00:23:49,700 --> 00:23:51,580
They didn't know what a camera looked like.

360
00:23:51,680 --> 00:23:53,780
All they knew is in a sniper scope

361
00:23:53,781 --> 00:23:56,440
that that's a weird looking thing.

362
00:23:57,320 --> 00:23:58,420
I thought I'd better shoot that guy.

363
00:23:59,060 --> 00:24:00,940
Military film and still cameramen

364
00:24:00,941 --> 00:24:02,660
covered the war in Vietnam as well,

365
00:24:03,460 --> 00:24:05,580
often changing reels in the midst of enemy fire.

366
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:09,020
I just kind of turned my back,

367
00:24:09,260 --> 00:24:10,460
crouched down behind something

368
00:24:10,461 --> 00:24:12,160
that I knew that would be good cover,

369
00:24:12,920 --> 00:24:14,160
crouched down there, sit down,

370
00:24:14,460 --> 00:24:18,980
and take the film out, wrap it quickly in the container, seal it up,

371
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:21,980
take the new roll out, and load it into the camera,

372
00:24:22,220 --> 00:24:23,800
load it properly where it would work right,

373
00:24:24,080 --> 00:24:28,580
and all the time doing this, pay attention to what's going on around you.

374
00:24:28,581 --> 00:24:35,920
The cameraman or camerawoman is the most valorous of all of journalists.

375
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:39,880
They've got to be out there in front where the action is

376
00:24:39,881 --> 00:24:41,020
or they don't get the pictures.

377
00:24:41,021 --> 00:24:47,020
For camera operators, maintaining equipment in Vietnam's hot, humid climate was a chore.

378
00:24:47,021 --> 00:24:52,020
Wind-up motion picture cameras, which were spring-driven, were often used

379
00:24:52,021 --> 00:24:56,020
because battery-operated cameras couldn't survive the heat and moisture of the jungle.

380
00:24:56,021 --> 00:24:59,020
The rainy season required special precautions as well.

381
00:24:59,021 --> 00:25:04,020
You had a little terrarium inside your camera if you didn't take good care of it.

382
00:25:04,021 --> 00:25:10,020
During the monsoon season, we used the Nikon underwater camera to shoot pictures.

383
00:25:10,021 --> 00:25:10,721
pictures.

384
00:25:10,722 --> 00:25:14,180
We were limited on lenses, but at least we could take pictures in the rain.

385
00:25:15,100 --> 00:25:20,320
The payoff for enduring Vietnam's harsh conditions was a rare opportunity to report from the

386
00:25:20,321 --> 00:25:22,392
front without government restrictions.

387
00:25:22,393 --> 00:25:25,500
Officials were reluctant to impose a system of censorship

388
00:25:25,501 --> 00:25:30,960
in a war that had never been formally declared, giving correspondents free reign to follow

389
00:25:30,961 --> 00:25:36,919
the troops. We were free to go to a unit, any unit we asked.

390
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:39,600
I don't remember ever being

391
00:25:39,601 --> 00:25:45,951
stopped from covering a unit. Reporters had the run of the battlefield.

392
00:25:45,952 --> 00:25:47,740
They could jump on a

393
00:25:47,741 --> 00:25:53,267
transport with troops and go anywhere they wanted in Vietnam.

394
00:25:53,268 --> 00:25:56,620
And there was that sense of welcoming

395
00:25:56,621 --> 00:26:01,860
American reporters to the battlefield I don't believe has ever been repeated,

396
00:26:01,861 --> 00:26:03,673
and I doubt it ever will.

397
00:26:03,674 --> 00:26:09,040
But frontline reporting's unlimited access led to stories which took issue

398
00:26:09,041 --> 00:26:11,000
with official claims of imminent victory,

399
00:26:11,740 --> 00:26:13,420
creating an adversarial relationship

400
00:26:13,421 --> 00:26:16,240
which reflected growing divisions in the country as a whole.

401
00:26:17,820 --> 00:26:21,620
Reporters attending press conferences in Saigon

402
00:26:21,621 --> 00:26:26,900
who had been to battle because it was an open war zone,

403
00:26:27,200 --> 00:26:29,400
they knew they were not telling the truth,

404
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:31,560
or at least the truth that they had seen.

405
00:26:32,420 --> 00:26:36,420
And so the conflict came to be called the credibility gap

406
00:26:36,421 --> 00:26:39,160
seemed to get worse and worse.

407
00:26:39,480 --> 00:26:41,620
The power and immediacy of television

408
00:26:41,621 --> 00:26:44,660
made its front-line coverage particularly difficult to refute.

409
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:47,820
This is what the war in Vietnam is all about.

410
00:26:48,540 --> 00:26:52,300
In 1965, a TV crew led by Morley Safer

411
00:26:52,301 --> 00:26:55,300
filmed a group of Marines setting a rural village ablaze.

412
00:26:56,040 --> 00:26:57,500
While the film was in transit,

413
00:26:58,420 --> 00:27:01,540
Safer's audio report was summarized on the CBS Evening News

414
00:27:01,541 --> 00:27:04,080
and promptly denied by the Pentagon.

415
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:08,040
Two days later, CBS aired the film on television,

416
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:10,020
triggering an uproar at the White House.

417
00:27:10,820 --> 00:27:14,280
Frank Stanton, who's the president of CBS,

418
00:27:14,400 --> 00:27:17,260
got a call from Lyndon Johnson the very next morning,

419
00:27:18,040 --> 00:27:20,800
and his quote was allegedly something like,

420
00:27:21,120 --> 00:27:23,560
Frank, what are you trying to do? Are you trying to screw me?

421
00:27:23,800 --> 00:27:25,440
But Lyndon Johnson didn't use the word screw

422
00:27:25,441 --> 00:27:27,540
because he was a very outspoken Texan.

423
00:27:28,180 --> 00:27:31,440
Despite White House pressure, CBS didn't back down.

424
00:27:32,120 --> 00:27:36,200
We were only writing, even through World War II, just writing about these things.

425
00:27:36,201 --> 00:27:42,340
It was easy for the military to deny the story, and we didn't have physical evidence.

426
00:27:42,840 --> 00:27:48,020
In this case, they suddenly realized they didn't have that defense any longer.

427
00:27:54,200 --> 00:27:57,140
After the Tet Offensive in early 1968,

428
00:27:58,020 --> 00:28:04,880
CBS anchorman Walter Cronkite went to Vietnam and filed his own report, concluding that victory was nowhere in sight.

429
00:28:05,140 --> 00:28:11,080
We have been too often disappointed by the optimism of the American leaders, both in Vietnam and Washington,

430
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:16,280
to have faith any longer in the silver linings they find in the darkest clouds.

431
00:28:17,140 --> 00:28:20,640
Watching the news that night, President Johnson told his aides,

432
00:28:21,060 --> 00:28:23,620
If I've lost Cronkite, I've lost Middle America.

433
00:28:24,460 --> 00:28:26,740
Four weeks later, he dropped a bombshell of his own.

434
00:28:27,140 --> 00:28:33,680
I will not accept the nomination of my party for another term as your president.

435
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:39,280
Now, that was the week, apparently, he was making up his decision that he wasn't going to run again.

436
00:28:39,281 --> 00:28:46,780
We know that that was the same week that he was besieging the Pentagon, demanding information.

437
00:28:46,960 --> 00:28:50,540
How could this thing happen when you were telling me we were winning the war?

438
00:28:50,740 --> 00:28:53,080
I had the same question in his heart that I had in mind.

439
00:28:53,081 --> 00:29:01,920
As in Crimea a century before, frontline reporting of an unpopular war through a popular medium had changed the political landscape.

440
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:08,680
Lyndon Johnson had enormous political problems with these kinds of eyewitness reports that tell people what's going on.

441
00:29:09,400 --> 00:29:12,520
The Vietnam War would continue for seven years.

442
00:29:12,521 --> 00:29:18,180
But by the time U.S. forces left Saigon amid televised scenes of chaos in 1975,

443
00:29:19,020 --> 00:29:24,020
many in the military believed that their open-door policy toward frontline reporting had been a mistake.

444
00:29:24,840 --> 00:29:27,540
It was one they were determined not to repeat.

445
00:29:31,100 --> 00:29:33,980
64 journalists died in the Vietnam War.

446
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:38,160
Approximately 50 U.S. correspondents died during World War II.

447
00:29:38,161 --> 00:29:42,560
Frontline reporting will return on Modern Marvels.

448
00:29:45,260 --> 00:29:50,860
In the wake of Vietnam, government officials strove to exert more control over frontline reporting.

449
00:29:51,660 --> 00:29:52,537
When the U.S.

450
00:29:52,538 --> 00:29:57,800
invaded Grenada in 1983, correspondents were kept off the island for 48 hours.

451
00:29:58,640 --> 00:30:01,420
That was a direct result of war coverage in Vietnam.

452
00:30:02,220 --> 00:30:04,800
I heard it over and over and over again.

453
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:06,900
Oh, we can't turn this into another Vietnam.

454
00:30:06,901 --> 00:30:10,080
There were many, many lessons attributed to that war.

455
00:30:10,680 --> 00:30:15,120
One of them, I believe, from the Pentagon was never give the press an open run again.

456
00:30:15,660 --> 00:30:20,260
But while officials tried to restrict war coverage, the market for that coverage was expanding,

457
00:30:20,660 --> 00:30:26,480
a trend underscored by the creation in 1980 of Ted Turner's 24-hour cable news channel, CNN.

458
00:30:27,480 --> 00:30:33,480
Ted Turner's charge to us was to go on the air with the mission of covering news

459
00:30:33,481 --> 00:30:39,020
wherever it broke and wherever we could cover it on the entire planet.

460
00:30:39,380 --> 00:30:43,260
They could be on the air faster because they were 24-7.

461
00:30:43,720 --> 00:30:46,020
Nobody else in those days was 24-7.

462
00:30:46,460 --> 00:30:47,620
No one else was real-time.

463
00:30:48,320 --> 00:30:50,820
CNN's search for live worldwide coverage

464
00:30:50,821 --> 00:30:53,860
coincided with the birth of commercial satellite providers

465
00:30:53,861 --> 00:30:58,440
like Pan Amsat, which launched its first satellite into orbit in 1985.

466
00:30:59,120 --> 00:31:01,320
Up until that point, it was all government operators

467
00:31:01,321 --> 00:31:05,280
because it costs so much, first of all, to put a satellite up into space,

468
00:31:05,400 --> 00:31:09,260
and secondly, it costs quite a bit just to have the ground infrastructures.

469
00:31:10,020 --> 00:31:12,740
And the first customer was CNN, Ted Turner.

470
00:31:17,820 --> 00:31:23,760
Commercial satellites are often launched into orbit around the equator, 22,300 miles from the ground.

471
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:28,940
At that distance, a satellite circles the Earth at 17,500 miles per hour,

472
00:31:29,520 --> 00:31:31,660
keeping pace with the rotation of the Earth itself.

473
00:31:33,160 --> 00:31:36,760
A satellite constellation of approximately 180 such units,

474
00:31:37,200 --> 00:31:40,420
spaced two degrees apart, provides near-global coverage.

475
00:31:42,220 --> 00:31:45,260
Companies like Pan-AMSAT track their movements from the ground

476
00:31:45,261 --> 00:31:49,180
to correct positioning and monitor interference from outside signals.

477
00:31:49,860 --> 00:31:54,280
If you're on Earth, you can always expect that satellite to be in that same relative location.

478
00:31:54,920 --> 00:31:57,760
But that requires a little bit of management

479
00:31:57,761 --> 00:32:02,760
because the gravitational forces tend to want to pull the satellite in different directions.

480
00:32:03,320 --> 00:32:06,800
Since satellites receive signals which have weakened over long distance,

481
00:32:07,600 --> 00:32:10,760
they must amplify those signals before transmitting them back to Earth.

482
00:32:11,240 --> 00:32:13,720
They do that by drawing power from the sun.

483
00:32:14,460 --> 00:32:16,960
One on my left here is called a spinner.

484
00:32:17,280 --> 00:32:19,080
It's sort of shaped like a canister.

485
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:22,560
And at any given time, the sun is illuminating one side.

486
00:32:23,560 --> 00:32:30,660
So that power charges the batteries on the spacecraft, and that's the power source.

487
00:32:31,360 --> 00:32:35,420
This is an HS601. It's a tri-axis stabilized satellite,

488
00:32:35,960 --> 00:32:39,320
and it has two solar panels that track the sun.

489
00:32:39,321 --> 00:32:45,160
So at all times, the full power is generated on the satellite.

490
00:32:45,400 --> 00:32:49,760
So it has the capacity almost twice that of the spinner model.

491
00:32:49,761 --> 00:32:57,226
CNN's financially risky strategy of global satellite coverage began to pay dividends in 1989 during the U.S.

492
00:32:57,227 --> 00:32:58,540
invasion of Panama.

493
00:32:59,320 --> 00:33:07,640
While credentialed reporters were still largely kept away from the fighting, CNN's round-the-clock coverage of its aftermath redefined television's role.

494
00:33:08,400 --> 00:33:11,540
The more we covered those stories, the higher our profile rose.

495
00:33:12,500 --> 00:33:15,640
Satellite television also helped create a global audience for CNN.

496
00:33:16,620 --> 00:33:20,140
That became evident in the buildup before the first Gulf War in 1991,

497
00:33:20,540 --> 00:33:26,560
when CNN convinced the Iraqi government to let it set up an exclusive four-wire phone connection in Baghdad

498
00:33:26,561 --> 00:33:30,580
that could send a radio signal to a satellite uplink in Amman, Jordan.

499
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:36,160
We spent roughly $14,000 a day to keep the four-wire up.

500
00:33:36,161 --> 00:33:44,260
And basically this simple instrument allowed us to overshoot the telephone exchange complex in Baghdad,

501
00:33:44,261 --> 00:33:51,060
government-run and government-owned, and we went by microwave from Baghdad to Amman,

502
00:33:51,200 --> 00:33:56,660
to our bureau in Amman, and from Amman up on the satellite, down on the satellite into headquarters

503
00:33:56,661 --> 00:34:00,581
in Atlanta, and then back up on the satellites and around the world.

504
00:34:00,582 --> 00:34:02,080
This is an updated version

505
00:34:02,081 --> 00:34:06,600
of the famous four-wire box that we heard a lot about during the first Gulf War with CNN.

506
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:09,608
It gives you an amplified speaker for the people from the U.S.

507
00:34:09,609 --> 00:34:11,080
to talk, and you hear out of there,

508
00:34:11,081 --> 00:34:15,160
and when you hit a talk button, it amplifies your voice down the four-wire line

509
00:34:15,161 --> 00:34:16,420
so they hear you back in the States.

510
00:34:16,960 --> 00:34:18,380
Two-way audio with amplification.

511
00:34:20,960 --> 00:34:23,440
If you're still with us, you're going to hear the bombs now.

512
00:34:23,520 --> 00:34:25,740
They are hitting the center of this city.

513
00:34:26,660 --> 00:34:31,460
When the United States began bombing Baghdad on the night of January 17, 1991,

514
00:34:32,040 --> 00:34:33,940
the city's phone system shut down,

515
00:34:34,580 --> 00:34:38,820
leaving only the CNN team of Bernard Shaw, John Holloman, and Peter Arnett

516
00:34:38,821 --> 00:34:42,380
with the ability to communicate the news, thanks to the 4-Wire.

517
00:34:42,960 --> 00:34:46,820
They also videotaped the bombing from the ninth floor of their hotel for future broadcasts.

518
00:34:47,960 --> 00:34:50,520
Millions of citizens around the world and their leaders

519
00:34:50,521 --> 00:34:55,200
tuned to CNN to hear their exclusive coverage of a war unfolding around them.

520
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:59,160
There's a very cool breeze blowing through the window here,

521
00:35:00,040 --> 00:35:03,460
and we are sweating in more ways than one.

522
00:35:03,461 --> 00:35:07,380
The situation, the environment, very desperate and very frightening.

523
00:35:07,960 --> 00:35:10,800
In war, one moment you're alive, the next moment you're dead.

524
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:14,880
We were quite aware that that could happen at any time.

525
00:35:15,140 --> 00:35:18,000
I mean, there were people dying in buildings across the street.

526
00:35:20,200 --> 00:35:21,220
Alone among U.S.

527
00:35:21,221 --> 00:35:27,340
networks, CNN continuously broadcast video via satellite from Baghdad in the days that followed.

528
00:35:28,100 --> 00:35:31,420
The first television coverage of a war from behind enemy lines.

529
00:35:31,940 --> 00:35:35,760
And a vivid demonstration of the growing primacy of satellite reporting.

530
00:35:36,600 --> 00:35:39,740
There were countries that took their national programming off the air

531
00:35:39,741 --> 00:35:41,560
and put CNN out in English

532
00:35:41,561 --> 00:35:46,560
because that was the most current, important thing

533
00:35:46,561 --> 00:35:48,280
that was happening in the world at the time.

534
00:35:48,960 --> 00:35:49,980
And everyone watched CNN.

535
00:35:50,360 --> 00:35:52,440
That's basically the only place you could see the coverage.

536
00:35:53,280 --> 00:35:57,860
Through that, I think a lot of cable news organizations

537
00:35:57,861 --> 00:36:00,620
realized that this is a major opportunity

538
00:36:00,621 --> 00:36:02,080
and satellites was the way to go.

539
00:36:02,780 --> 00:36:06,700
But for network correspondents stationed in Saudi Arabia during the Gulf War,

540
00:36:07,240 --> 00:36:09,260
satellites still posed logistical problems.

541
00:36:09,760 --> 00:36:13,820
The uplink apparatus weighed hundreds of pounds and required hours to set up,

542
00:36:14,220 --> 00:36:19,320
making it impractical for transport and giving rise to the phenomenon of rooftop journalism,

543
00:36:19,640 --> 00:36:22,380
in which correspondents were all but tethered to their posts.

544
00:36:23,160 --> 00:36:25,840
It would take maybe three hours to set all the gear up,

545
00:36:26,260 --> 00:36:30,980
take the pieces of the dish, put them together, lock them together, put them on a stand.

546
00:36:30,981 --> 00:36:38,540
Then the camera crews and reporters would have to go out into the field, gather their tape and their stories, bring it back to you, and then we would transmit it.

547
00:36:39,580 --> 00:36:45,744
Correspondents also chafed over government restrictions that kept most of the 1,400 accredited reporters stationed with U.S.

548
00:36:45,745 --> 00:36:48,080
forces from accompanying troops into the field.

549
00:36:48,700 --> 00:36:53,880
To help fill the gap, the network's often aired footage of airstrikes provided by the Department of Defense.

550
00:36:54,640 --> 00:37:01,660
One of the drawbacks of the initial Gulf War was that you saw it through the lens of a nose camera of a bomb or an aircraft.

551
00:37:02,140 --> 00:37:07,380
What you didn't see was what happened the moment that bomb or that transmission went to hash.

552
00:37:08,560 --> 00:37:12,240
It was not freedom to cover what you wanted.

553
00:37:12,900 --> 00:37:14,900
You were watched very carefully.

554
00:37:15,360 --> 00:37:20,400
We'd have sometimes three information officers out with a unit that we were with.

555
00:37:20,401 --> 00:37:24,940
And they watched and sat next to the person being interviewed, three feet away.

556
00:37:25,340 --> 00:37:29,040
It wasn't direct censorship. It was indirect.

557
00:37:29,440 --> 00:37:32,780
It almost came to a constitutional violation,

558
00:37:33,400 --> 00:37:37,460
the way they kept us from reporting what our troops were doing in action,

559
00:37:38,140 --> 00:37:44,000
and by so doing, denied to the American people the information which was most essential to them

560
00:37:44,001 --> 00:37:47,640
in making their decisions on whether this was the right thing to do or not.

561
00:37:47,641 --> 00:37:54,400
By war's end, many reporters voiced fears that heavy censorship was becoming a permanent impediment to their job.

562
00:37:54,401 --> 00:38:02,880
But a return engagement in Iraq 12 years later would mark yet another sea change in the treatment and the technology of frontline reporting.

563
00:38:04,220 --> 00:38:11,740
In the 1991 Gulf War, CNN reported the bombing of Baghdad 27 minutes before it was confirmed by the Pentagon.

564
00:38:12,760 --> 00:38:15,220
Frontline reporting will return on Modern Marvels.

565
00:38:17,640 --> 00:38:19,881
For two decades after Vietnam, U.S.

566
00:38:19,882 --> 00:38:25,580
policymakers and frontline reporters often viewed each other with suspicion and distrust.

567
00:38:26,420 --> 00:38:36,184
But by the mid-1990s, more portable communications tools, coupled with the rise of foreign satellite networks, like Qatar's Al Jazeera, forced the military and the

568
00:38:36,185 --> 00:38:38,400
media to re-examine the relationship.

569
00:38:40,520 --> 00:38:43,740
There's only so much that censorship can do anymore because of the technology.

570
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:49,160
You can file a story from a very difficult location that before would have been simply impossible.

571
00:38:49,880 --> 00:38:52,380
The Pentagon is not alone.

572
00:38:53,220 --> 00:38:54,880
Something happens in the desert.

573
00:38:55,180 --> 00:38:58,500
Al Jazeera spins it one way, the Pentagon spins it the other.

574
00:38:58,740 --> 00:39:04,300
They needed a counter to Al Jazeera that was reliable and believable.

575
00:39:04,301 --> 00:39:10,700
Hearkening to the policy of World War II, the Pentagon embedded about 600 correspondents

576
00:39:10,701 --> 00:39:11,401
with U.S.

577
00:39:11,402 --> 00:39:16,320
troops during Operation Iraqi Freedom in 2003, while imposing minimal ground rules

578
00:39:16,321 --> 00:39:18,120
to protect their security.

579
00:39:18,121 --> 00:39:23,860
One result was an unprecedented array of real-time coverage from a surprising source.

580
00:39:23,861 --> 00:39:26,540
Still photographers.

581
00:39:26,541 --> 00:39:31,740
Digital cameras allow photographers to take pictures, examine and edit them on a laptop,

582
00:39:31,741 --> 00:39:36,240
transport them across the globe within minutes, a radical departure from the strictures of

583
00:39:36,241 --> 00:39:38,660
the past. Everyone was working in digital.

584
00:39:38,661 --> 00:39:41,080
The whole point of taking digital is being

585
00:39:41,081 --> 00:39:44,140
able to transmit those images back in a timely fashion.

586
00:39:44,141 --> 00:39:45,920
And you end up taking a lot more

587
00:39:45,921 --> 00:39:48,249
than you would if you were just shooting film.

588
00:39:48,250 --> 00:39:50,680
This is a basic digital camera, the professional

589
00:39:50,681 --> 00:39:53,509
one we use, and I would shoot images on here.

590
00:39:53,510 --> 00:39:56,400
At the end of the day, I would take my digital

591
00:39:56,401 --> 00:40:03,460
card out which keep about 400 images on there and you throw that into a reader on my computer

592
00:40:03,461 --> 00:40:10,940
edit on there and once I've edited that on hook up using this device which uses radio waves

593
00:40:10,941 --> 00:40:15,940
communicate to satellite over my area that's a major advantage is you know shooting digital

594
00:40:15,941 --> 00:40:20,380
seeing your pictures right away being able to adjust your exposure you know knowing that you

595
00:40:20,381 --> 00:40:25,368
got the picture and deciding kind of on the spot what to transmit.

596
00:40:25,369 --> 00:40:26,880
Twenty minutes after

597
00:40:26,881 --> 00:40:31,820
a picture is made, we can have that distributed to publications all over the world.

598
00:40:32,640 --> 00:40:37,780
Another digital tool, the mini-disc, allows photographers to record and transmit interviews

599
00:40:37,781 --> 00:40:39,660
from the field to augment their pictures.

600
00:40:40,860 --> 00:40:45,400
If Ben's walking around with a mini-disc and a microphone, he can both interview his subjects,

601
00:40:45,401 --> 00:40:47,200
He can also do personal journals.

602
00:40:47,820 --> 00:40:49,400
And then we can create these animated sequences

603
00:40:49,960 --> 00:40:53,860
where we're actually stringing pictures together, creating a narrative spine.

604
00:40:54,620 --> 00:40:56,080
This is Ben Lowy in Iraq.

605
00:40:56,300 --> 00:40:59,920
After an initial period of realizing it was some kind of terrorist attack,

606
00:41:00,020 --> 00:41:01,380
people started taking positions.

607
00:41:01,780 --> 00:41:05,480
And I just grabbed my cameras and started photographing the evacuation of the wounded.

608
00:41:05,940 --> 00:41:09,540
When you're surrounded by the members of your unit and their tight-knit groups,

609
00:41:09,620 --> 00:41:11,520
that is your family while you're deployed.

610
00:41:11,940 --> 00:41:13,800
And that's a bond of friendship.

611
00:41:13,801 --> 00:41:22,920
If we're taking that still imagery and making video with the audio and the still imagery, we've all of a sudden hit the broadcast space.

612
00:41:23,120 --> 00:41:32,250
Pretty soon it'll just be one person with one camera who will be the talent, the reporter, the producer, and the cameraman, and the editor all at once because we have

613
00:41:32,251 --> 00:41:33,680
the technology to do that.

614
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:38,080
Smaller and lighter equipment will always be welcome on the front lines of war,

615
00:41:38,740 --> 00:41:43,900
ensuring that even state-of-the-art innovations, like the Bloom Mobile, are fated to become obsolete.

616
00:41:43,901 --> 00:41:48,660
The challenge that we've been issued by the Department of Defense is to try and get even smaller,

617
00:41:48,940 --> 00:41:53,900
such that our packages, in fact, could mount directly on military vehicles

618
00:41:53,901 --> 00:41:58,040
without having to take a civilian vehicle onto the battlefield.

619
00:41:59,360 --> 00:42:03,220
Real-time war coverage has changed the ground rules for networks as well.

620
00:42:03,680 --> 00:42:07,540
While broadcasting the advance of U.S. troops toward Baghdad in 2003,

621
00:42:08,680 --> 00:42:14,040
CNN imposed a time delay to restrict the potential sudden appearance of gruesome images from the battlefield.

622
00:42:15,640 --> 00:42:20,900
More producers and directors and editors were trained in the use of time delay, a seven-second time delay.

623
00:42:21,480 --> 00:42:23,640
Why? Not because we wanted to censor the images,

624
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:29,440
but because the images that might have appeared on air into the households of millions of people around the world,

625
00:42:29,760 --> 00:42:31,060
we had absolutely no control over.

626
00:42:31,061 --> 00:42:35,180
For reporters in the field, the physical risks are more immediate.

627
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:41,874
In 2001, more journalists were killed during the war in Afghanistan than were U.S.

628
00:42:41,875 --> 00:42:42,575
soldiers.

629
00:42:43,380 --> 00:42:48,620
Eleven more correspondents, including David Bloom, died within three weeks in Iraq.

630
00:42:50,120 --> 00:42:55,260
These were very courageous colleagues, hundreds of them, some of whom did not come back.

631
00:42:55,680 --> 00:42:59,720
And that's the ultimate sacrifice in pursuit of free speech.

632
00:42:59,721 --> 00:43:06,480
The misconception among many people is that war correspondents have a very glamorous job.

633
00:43:07,040 --> 00:43:11,680
It's not glamorous at all. It's very, very potentially deadly.

634
00:43:16,360 --> 00:43:23,760
There's no shortage of reasons why frontline reporters risk life and limb in the world's most dangerous zones,

635
00:43:24,360 --> 00:43:27,340
while bearing witness to extreme displays of heroism.

636
00:43:27,341 --> 00:43:30,020
and horror.

637
00:43:32,420 --> 00:43:37,400
Korea, love of journalism, love of fear.

638
00:43:38,300 --> 00:43:39,720
Want to push the bubble a little.

639
00:43:41,940 --> 00:43:46,080
It is so exciting, it's so action-packed,

640
00:43:46,081 --> 00:43:48,380
and it's noisy, and it's horrible.

641
00:43:49,640 --> 00:43:51,960
You see guys getting shot and getting hit,

642
00:43:52,180 --> 00:43:56,120
and you take pictures of them for record.

643
00:43:56,121 --> 00:44:01,260
Maybe we can learn from it, so we're not always doomed to repeat it, though we often are.

644
00:44:01,920 --> 00:44:09,240
And maybe it's a folly, but it's important to keep that going.

645
00:44:10,160 --> 00:44:16,980
For close to two centuries, correspondents have grown ever more dependent on an expanding array of technological devices

646
00:44:16,981 --> 00:44:21,620
to deliver the news of distant battles from the front lines of war.

647
00:44:21,621 --> 00:44:29,000
For without those memes and those media, there would often be no messages.

